[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug:[22m The Rust translation used [38;5;10mrem_euclid(2.0)[0m to translate C's [38;5;10mfmodf(h / 60.0f, 2)[0m, but these have different semantics for negative inputs:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- C's [38;5;10mfmodf[0m preserves the sign of the dividend. For example, [38;5;10mfmodf(-0.5, 2.0)[0m returns [38;5;10m-0.5[0m.[0m[0m
- Rust's [38;5;10mrem_euclid[0m always returns a non-negative result. [38;5;10m(-0.5f32).rem_euclid(2.0)[0m returns [38;5;10m1.5[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This caused the intermediate value [38;5;10mx[0m to be computed differently when the hue [38;5;10mh[0m is negative (test vectors 43 and 61 both had negative hue values like [38;5;10m-30[0m and [38;5;10m-10[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Replaced [38;5;10m.rem_euclid(2.0)[0m with [38;5;10m% 2.0[0m. Rust's [38;5;10m%[0m operator on floats matches C's [38;5;10mfmodf[0m behavior — it preserves the sign of the dividend, producing identical results to the original C code.[0m[0m
